    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"989743\">Jack McCarthy</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/583339-AI-authored-projects/topic/Counterexample.20to.20Dinitz-Garg-Goemans.20conjecture/near/612785907\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>However, it is quite likely that this project could help expose some missing infrastructure for flows in Mathlib's graph theory library.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>\"expose some missing infrastructure\"? We don't have flows at all <span aria-label=\"sweat smile\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f605\" role=\"img\" title=\"sweat smile\">:sweat_smile:</span> (and we're aware of it)</p>\n<p>I don't think AI authored projects are the solution, flows require some careful planning: What about preflows? Multi-commodity flows? Should we define it for every <code>SimpleGraph</code> or just the complete graph? Should it be a subtype of <code>G.EdgeLabeling</code>? Which typeclasses should the value type have (<code>Comm</code>/<code>Zero</code>/<code>Add</code>/<code>Neg</code>/<code>AddGroup</code>/<code>LinearOrder</code>/<code>IsOrderedAddMonoid</code>)? Which of these decisions lead to more ergonomic proofs down the line, and cover all the common use-cases in the literature? And surely CSLib should also have a say here</p>\n<p>I do think LLMs could help investigate each of these questions, but humans should make the judgements.<br>\nIf anyone is interested, AFAIK no one is (publicly) working on these and hopefully someone takes this up!</p>",
        "id": 612793019,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1785041642
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"989743\">Jack McCarthy</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/583339-AI-authored-projects/topic/Counterexample.20to.20Dinitz-Garg-Goemans.20conjecture/near/612785907\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>there are potentially some missing definitions in Mathlib to state this idiomatically</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Thank you, Jack, that's fair. I'm happy for it to remain a standalone repo (comparator layout as you suggested, so it is mechanically checkable). The close read is really helpful.</p>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"933054\">@Snir Broshi</span>  yes, I agree on all counts. The flows design needs human judgment and community planning, it isn't something to pick up here, except perhaps as a case study. I'll keep it available as a worked example in case it is ever useful.</p>",
        "id": 612829346,
        "sender_full_name": "Jason Hickey",
        "timestamp": 1785080719
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"1176560\">@Jason Hickey</span> , you can submit your formalization to <a href=\"https://github.com/Vilin97/lean-pool\">lean pool</a>.  While Mathlib moves slowly but ends up with high-quality definitions, lean pool moves much faster and is more okay with imperfect but still useful API.</p>",
